`Comments.js` does what the goal asks.\n- The Toolbar now waits for a `comments:view:built` notification before it wires the add button, so the Comments build no longer runs during loading.\n- A button wired late gets added to `paragraphControls`/`lockControls`, and it copies the toolbar comment button's `keepState` and disabled state. That fixes the stale disabled state from attempt 2.\n- The differential oracle passes on all 4 cases.\n\n**Why I'm rejecting:**\n1. **No measured gain.** The packet exists to move the Comments build out of the open window and make opening faster. On the quiet VM the executor measured Ready at −40 ms median, with a middle-50% range of [−123…+34]. LoadDoc was −40 ms [−107…+68]. Both ranges cross zero, and B was faster in only 19 of 32 pairs. A difference that small against the spread of repeated runs of identical code is not a gain. The single differential run doesn't help either: `open` was slower on text.docx (+604 ms), styles.docx (+203 ms) and simple.rtf (+120 ms), and faster only on plain.txt (−171 ms).\n2. **Lost lock test.** The lock fix for the late-wired button rests on a behaviour found by `perf/comments-addnew-locks.cjs`: after load, the button stayed disabled. That test was never committed and is lost. The contract says the \"Add-comment toolbar button locks behave as before\". The differential oracle passed with that bug present, so it doesn't check this. Without a committed test, the behaviour behind the fix isn't checked anywhere.\n\n**To fix:**\n- Show a Ready/LoadDoc gain whose range across pairs doesn't cross zero. That means more pairs, or measuring the Comments build's cost directly inside the open window. If no real gain exists, record that in `baseline.md` and close the packet as no gain.\n- Rewrite and commit the after-load lock test under `.theseus/oracle/perf/`. It should cover the state after load and the lostConnect and paragraphLock cases.\n\nVERDICT: REJECT no measured gain beyond run-to-run spread (Ready −4